# Reflection: Session 4

22 August 2026 | Gangrade Pranjal Jitendra | 2025MB26023

---

Do not default to a foundation model. Start at the lowest rung that solves the job. Rules, then retrieval, then predictive models, then generative or agentic.

We applied this to our own capability list the same week and it improved the product. Hard limits on burn, contract dates and severity counts are rules. They were inside the agent's scope only because everything was. Pulling them out and feeding them in as fixed inputs made the agent's job smaller and gave us deterministic signals to measure it against. What is left over is genuinely hard: reading twenty five engagements of written narrative a week and ranking what deserves attention. I can now say that with a straight face because we tried the lower rungs first and can explain why they fail. That may be the difference between a defensible AI product and an AI product.

**The question I could not answer.** If experts stop applying their own minds and just accept the AI, the risk returns to the product owner. Our entire value proposition is that the manager reads less. The thing I most fear is a worsening engagement ranked low, the manager trusting the rank, and the problem growing unseen. That is worse than doing nothing, because we replaced a shallow review with no review.

I had this as a risk. The session made it a design constraint. The faculty's pattern from his lending product is human and AI deciding in parallel, agreement passing silently, disagreement forcing the human to justify. We cannot use it directly, because the parallel human decision is the cost we are removing. On a sample rather than the whole portfolio it might work, and that is now in our design as blind weeks. I am not confident it is enough. I am confident it beats a disclaimer.

**Where I disagree with the class.** A participant asked about automating bug triage, and the discussion split between giving people back an hour and people suspecting headcount cuts. There is a third case neither named. Sometimes the task is how the person builds the judgement they need later. A manager who reads twenty five status reports badly is still absorbing pattern and tone. Hand them a ranked briefing and they get conclusions without immersion. The product may degrade the expertise it depends on. I have stopped calling those five hours pure waste.

**Adoption.** Resistance is a rational response to switching cost, not irrationality. My rollout plans have been training plus communication, which address awareness and not the fact that someone has a working method today. Our positioning risk is worse than usual: if delivery teams think an AI is checking their honesty, reports get vaguer and the signal we depend on disappears. Framing it as routing attention rather than monitoring people is right, and it will not survive one incident where someone is confronted with a sentence they wrote.

**The line I kept.** Tell me how you measure me and I will tell you how I behave. Measure the manager on flags cleared and they will clear flags.
